Yes, you can package a full root filesystem as a compressed tarball (tar.zst or tar.gz) in S3 and extract it onto a fresh Linux VM at boot, making your golden images 100% independent of cloud providers and regions.
This pattern—often called a root-tarball overlay or Pivot-Root / chroot deployment—eliminates provider snapshot APIs altogether while still avoiding slow apt-get and compilation steps.

How the S3 Root-Tarball Workflow Operates


1. Capture the Golden Root Filesystem (capture_golden_tarball.sh)

Once a build completes and all packages (Nginx, PHP/Tomcat, firewalls, helpers) are installed, strip out machine-specific state and stream the root filesystem straight into S3:
sh
#!/bin/sh
set -e

ARCHIVE_NAME="adt-golden-${STACK_TYPE}-$(/bin/date -u '+%Y%m%d').tar.zst"
TMP_ARCHIVE="/tmp/${ARCHIVE_NAME}"

# 1. Clean transient state so the image is completely generic
/usr/bin/apt-get clean
/bin/rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/* /tmp/* /var/tmp/*
/bin/rm -f /etc/ssh/ssh_host_* /etc/machine-id /var/lib/dbus/machine-id
/usr/bin/truncate -s 0 /var/log/*.log /var/log/*/*.log 2>/dev/null || true

# 2. Archive the root filesystem while excluding virtual/kernel mounts and network config
/bin/tar --numeric-owner --xattrs --acls \
        --exclude='/proc/*' \
        --exclude='/sys/*' \
        --exclude='/dev/*' \
        --exclude='/run/*' \
        --exclude='/tmp/*' \
        --exclude='/mnt/*' \
        --exclude='/media/*' \
        --exclude='/boot/*' \
        --exclude='/etc/fstab' \
        --exclude='/etc/network/*' \
        --exclude='/etc/netplan/*' \
        --exclude='/etc/systemd/network/*' \
        --exclude='/etc/udev/rules.d/70-persistent-net.rules' \
        --exclude="${TMP_ARCHIVE}" \
        -cpf - / | /usr/bin/zstd -T0 -6 -o "${TMP_ARCHIVE}"

# 3. Upload to your S3 image library bucket
/usr/local/bin/s3cmd put "${TMP_ARCHIVE}" "s3://adt-golden-images/${ARCHIVE_NAME}"
/bin/rm -f "${TMP_ARCHIVE}"
Use code with caution.
• Why excluding /boot, /etc/fstab, and /etc/netplan is critical: Every cloud provider uses its own kernel, disk UUIDs (/dev/sda vs /dev/vda), and network initialisation rules. Keeping the target VM's native /boot, /etc/fstab, and network files intact ensures the machine always boots cleanly on Linode, Hetzner, DigitalOcean, Vultr, or AWS.

2. Hydrate a Fresh VM from S3 at Boot (cloud-init Bootstrap)

When provisioning a new webserver on any cloud provider, boot a minimal stock Ubuntu/Debian image and run a tiny cloud-init script:
1. Pull and extract the tarball into a staging root (/mnt/newroot) or directly over the live filesystem:
	• Cleanest approach (chroot / pivot_root or overlay extraction): Download adt-golden-php.tar.zst from S3, extract it over / (preserving /boot, /etc/fstab, and /etc/netplan), regenerate SSH host keys (dpkg-reconfigure openssh-server) and /etc/machine-id (systemd-machine-id-setup), and reboot once into the hydrated OS.
	• Even simpler (Application & Service Stack Tarball): Instead of archiving the entire OS root /, archive only /usr/local, /opt, /etc/nginx, /etc/php (or /opt/tomcat), /var/www, and /var/lib/dpkg—or export your installed package cache (/var/cache/apt/archives/*.deb) plus /opt and /etc into S3. Extracting a stack tarball onto a stock base VM requires no reboot and avoids kernel/initramfs edge cases completely.

Trade-Offs to Watch For


Consideration	Full Root Tarball (/)	Stack + .deb Cache Tarball (/opt, /etc, .debs)
Boot Speed	Very fast (~30–45s including 1 reboot)	Fast (~45–60s, zero reboot needed)
Cross-Provider Safety	High, provided /boot, /etc/fstab, and network configs are excluded	Highest—uses the provider's native base OS untouched
Library Size in S3	~350 MB – 600 MB compressed (zstd)	~80 MB – 200 MB compressed (zstd)
Kernel / Driver Mismatches	Possible if moving across wildly different architectures (x86_64 vs ARM64)	None (native kernel and drivers remain intact)


Practical Recommendation for ADT

Store your archives in S3 keyed by architecture and stack—for example:
s3://adt-golden-images/x86_64/ubuntu-24.04-tomcat10.tar.zst
That gives you a single, cloud-agnostic image library that works identically across Linode, Hetzner, DigitalOcean, Vultr, and AWS with zero snapshot replication or region lock-in.
Would you like to test the full root tarball (/ + reboot) approach or the stack + .deb cache overlay (no reboot) approach first? I can write the exact cloud-init hydration snippet for whichever you prefer.

The Stack + .deb Cache Overlay approach is the safest, cleanest way to get near-snapshot boot speeds across any cloud provider or region with zero reboots and zero kernel/network conflicts.
Because every .deb package is already downloaded inside the archive, dpkg / apt-get installs your entire stack offline from local disk in seconds rather than hitting remote Ubuntu/Debian mirrors.

1. Capture Script (capture_stack_bundle.sh)

Run this once at the end of a clean build (or whenever you update your base PHP/Tomcat stack) to package /var/cache/apt/archives/*.deb, /usr/local, /opt, and your service configs into a single compressed zstd archive in S3:
sh
#!/bin/sh
###################################################################################
# @file        capture_stack_bundle.sh
# @brief       Packages installed .deb archives, binaries, and stack configs to S3.
# @author      Peter Winter
# @date        15/11/2026
# @version     1.0
###################################################################################
set -e

if [ -r /home/buildhome.dat ]
then
        BUILD_HOME="$(/bin/cat /home/buildhome.dat)"
else
        /bin/echo "Application root configuration file missing at /home/buildhome.dat" >&2
        exit 1
fi

STACK_TYPE="${1:-php}"
ARCH="$(/usr/bin/uname -m)"
OS_CODENAME="$(. /etc/os-release && /bin/echo "${VERSION_CODENAME}")"
BUNDLE_NAME="adt-stack-${OS_CODENAME}-${ARCH}-${STACK_TYPE}.tar.zst"
TMP_STAGE="/tmp/adt_bundle_stage"
TMP_ARCHIVE="/tmp/${BUNDLE_NAME}"

/bin/rm -rf "${TMP_STAGE}" "${TMP_ARCHIVE}"
/bin/mkdir -p "${TMP_STAGE}/debs" "${TMP_STAGE}/overlay"

# 1. Record exact list of manually installed packages
/usr/bin/apt-mark showmanual > "${TMP_STAGE}/manual_packages.list"

# 2. Copy cached .deb files (enable APT::Keep-Downloaded-Packages="true" during initial build)
if [ -d /var/cache/apt/archives ]
then
        /bin/cp -a /var/cache/apt/archives/*.deb "${TMP_STAGE}/debs/" 2>/dev/null || true
fi

# 3. Capture custom binaries (/usr/local, /opt) and base service configs (excluding node-specific secrets)
/bin/tar --numeric-owner \
        --exclude='*.log' \
        --exclude='/etc/nginx/sites-enabled/*' \
        --exclude='/etc/letsencrypt/*' \
        --exclude='/usr/local/bin/s3cmd*' \
        -cpf - /usr/local /opt /etc/nginx /etc/php /etc/systemd/system 2>/dev/null | /bin/tar -xpf - -C "${TMP_STAGE}/overlay" || true

# 4. Compress the complete bundle using multi-threaded zstd
/bin/tar -C "${TMP_STAGE}" -cpf - . | /usr/bin/zstd -T0 -6 -o "${TMP_ARCHIVE}"

# 5. Upload to S3 image registry bucket
/usr/local/bin/s3cmd put "${TMP_ARCHIVE}" "s3://adt-golden-images/${OS_CODENAME}/${ARCH}/${BUNDLE_NAME}"

# 6. Clean up temporary staging files
/bin/rm -rf "${TMP_STAGE}" "${TMP_ARCHIVE}"
/bin/echo "Success: Stack bundle uploaded to s3://adt-golden-images/${OS_CODENAME}/${ARCH}/${BUNDLE_NAME}"
exit 0
Use code with caution.
(Note: Before running your initial build that populates /var/cache/apt/archives/, drop Binary::apt::APT::Keep-Downloaded-Packages "true"; into /etc/apt/apt.conf.d/99keep-debs so apt-get retains the .deb files after installation).

2. Hydration Script (hydrate_from_bundle.sh)

Run this inside cloud-init on any newly booted node (Linode, Hetzner, DigitalOcean, Vultr, or AWS). If the bundle exists in S3, it installs the entire stack offline in seconds; if not, it falls back cleanly to your standard apt-get build scripts:
sh
#!/bin/sh
###################################################################################
# @file        hydrate_from_bundle.sh
# @brief       Downloads and applies a pre-packaged ADT stack + .deb bundle from S3.
# @author      Peter Winter
# @date        15/11/2026
# @version     1.0
###################################################################################
set -e

STACK_TYPE="${1:-php}"
ARCH="$(/usr/bin/uname -m)"
OS_CODENAME="$(. /etc/os-release && /bin/echo "${VERSION_CODENAME}")"
BUNDLE_NAME="adt-stack-${OS_CODENAME}-${ARCH}-${STACK_TYPE}.tar.zst"
S3_URI="s3://adt-golden-images/${OS_CODENAME}/${ARCH}/${BUNDLE_NAME}"
TMP_STAGE="/tmp/adt_hydrate"
TMP_ARCHIVE="/tmp/${BUNDLE_NAME}"

# Check if a matching golden bundle exists in S3; exit 2 to trigger standard apt fallback if absent
if ! /usr/local/bin/s3cmd info "${S3_URI}" >/dev/null 2>&1
then
        /bin/echo "No pre-built stack bundle found at ${S3_URI}. Falling back to standard build." >&2
        exit 2
fi

/bin/rm -rf "${TMP_STAGE}" "${TMP_ARCHIVE}"
/bin/mkdir -p "${TMP_STAGE}"

# 1. Pull and unpack the zstd bundle
/usr/local/bin/s3cmd get "${S3_URI}" "${TMP_ARCHIVE}"
/usr/bin/zstd -d -c "${TMP_ARCHIVE}" | /bin/tar -xpf - -C "${TMP_STAGE}"

# 2. Populate local apt cache and install all packages offline without hitting network mirrors
if [ -d "${TMP_STAGE}/debs" ] && [ -n "$(/bin/ls -A "${TMP_STAGE}/debs" 2>/dev/null)" ]
then
        /bin/mkdir -p /var/cache/apt/archives
        /bin/cp -a "${TMP_STAGE}/debs/"*.deb /var/cache/apt/archives/
        export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive
        /usr/bin/dpkg -i --force-confold /var/cache/apt/archives/*.deb || /usr/bin/apt-get -f install -y --no-download
fi

# 3. Overlay pre-configured /usr/local, /opt (e.g. Tomcat/Java), and base service templates
if [ -d "${TMP_STAGE}/overlay" ]
then
        /bin/tar -C "${TMP_STAGE}/overlay" -cpf - . | /bin/tar -xpf - -C /
        /bin/systemctl daemon-reload
fi

# 4. Clean up staging artefacts
/bin/rm -rf "${TMP_STAGE}" "${TMP_ARCHIVE}"
/bin/echo "Success: Node hydrated cleanly from ${BUNDLE_NAME}."
exit 0
Use code with caution.

Why This Fits ADT's Architecture

• Zero Provider or Region Lock-In: The exact same adt-stack-noble-x86_64-tomcat.tar.zst archive in S3 hydrates a fresh VM on Linode London, Hetzner Falkenstein, or Vultr Amsterdam identically.
• Automatic Graceful Fallback: If hydrate_from_bundle.sh returns exit code 2 (for example, on a brand-new OS release or CPU architecture where you haven't captured a bundle yet), your bootstrap script simply runs its normal apt-get path and captures a new bundle at the end.
• Multi-Stack Ready: Adding Tomcat/Java, Node.js, or PostgreSQL is just a matter of capturing adt-stack-noble-x86_64-tomcat.tar.zst once into your S3 bucket.
If you want to integrate this into your build flow, let me know whether you want hydrate_from_bundle.sh to use s3cmd or aws s3 (matching your existing ADT datastore driver), and I can adapt the S3 commands accordingly.


